<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baudouin of Belgium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as King Baudouin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baudouin was King of the Belgians from 1951 to 1993, succeeding his father Leopold III, who abdicated after the Royal Question. His reign saw the independence of the Belgian Congo in 1960 and the transformation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> into a federal state. In 1990 he was briefly declared unable to reign so that a law liberalising abortion could pass without his signature. He was succeeded by his brother Albert II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baudouin-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Baudouin of Belgium" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Baudouin_of_Belgium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baudouin was born on 7 September 1930 at Laeken, north of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the elder son of Prince Leopold and Princess Astrid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">King Albert I</a> was killed in a climbing accident in 1934, and Leopold became king. His mother died in a car accident in 1935, and in 1941 his father married Mary Lilian Baels. Germany invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in May 1940, and Leopold surrendered on 28 May and stayed under house arrest. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">Normandy landings</a> the family was deported to Germany and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and was freed in May 1945.</p><p>The Royal Question, over whether Leopold had collaborated, kept the family out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It rested on his surrender, his refusal to join the government in exile, his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> in November 1940 and his marriage to Lilian. Prince Charles served as regent, and the family lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A referendum in March 1950 narrowly favoured Leopold's return, but parliamentary dissent and public protests forced him to delegate his powers to Baudouin on 11 August 1950 and then to abdicate. Baudouin took the oath on 17 July 1951. In 1960 he declared the Belgian Congo independent. At the ceremony he praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> as a genius, and the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> answered with a speech citing the killings, insults and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> the Congolese had suffered under Belgian rule.</p><p>Katanga Province seceded with Belgian support, and Lumumba was executed there on 17 January 1961. A Belgian parliamentary investigation of 2001 concluded that Baudouin knew of the plot against Lumumba and did nothing to oppose it, though it found no conclusive evidence that he ordered its details. Baudouin married Doña Fabiola de Mora y Aragón in 1960; they had no children. In 1990 he refused to sign a law liberalising abortion, and the government declared him temporarily unable to reign for a day so that it could sign the bill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Baudouin died of a heart attack on 31 July 1993 at Motril in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, after a reign of 42 years. About 500,000 people came to see his body in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. He was buried at the Church of Our Lady of Laeken, and his brother succeeded him as Albert II. The King Baudouin Foundation, formed in 1976 on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> jubilee, works to improve living conditions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baudouin of Belgium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was succeeded by <b>Baudouin</b> (1951–1993), Albert II (1993–2013) and Philippe, king since 2013.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château of Stuyvenberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Motril, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.89,4.35&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Château of Stuyvenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Baudouin of Belgium"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5234 5768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5234 5768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.89,4.35&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
